{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/370077-shadowland-physics","id":370077,"title":"shadowland physics","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Wraith: The Oblivion"],"posts":[{"date":"2015-01-14T13:20:30-08:00","id":370077,"title":"shadowland physics","content":"If I can pass through the wall without falling through the floor, clearly I can be incorporeal with regard to some things and not others. Can I reach up through the roof of the car and pull myself onto the roof, then? (My answer would be yes, to err on the side of permissiveness and creativity -- but I'm wondering what others have done.)<br /> <br /> Can I voluntarily fall through the floor only if/when I want to? Will I fall through it even if I don't want to, assuming I get disoriented and forget which way is up (say, because something picked me up and threw me)?<br /> <br /> I think it was Crowley that mentioned a maximum thickness of two meters of dirt/stone that can impede progress. Is that a house rule? I don't remember seeing anything canonical addressing it, but it makes sense to have something preventing everyone from falling to the center of the earth.<br /> <br /> I also saw some mention of the oceans being solid (I forget which thread). Is that canonical? Just wondering. Seems to me at odds with rain falling harmlessly through you, for example.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4448-bphillips","name":"bphillips","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4448_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"30"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-14T18:34:54-08:00","id":370521,"title":"","content":"In Elysium, ancient Vampires did not lack in intelligence but long centuries left them with some mental rigidity. Becoming a vampire doesn't change the laws of physics in relationship to the corporal body and how it interacts with the physical world. For Wraiths, a young wraith is filled with the trauma of their recent death, possible derangement from the death and other events in their life, and coping mechanisms for the grief/stress of their new &quot;life&quot; all derived from the education in and experience of the Skinlands. The question isn't a matter of what physics apply, but rather within those physics - what limited perceptions does their damaged psyche conceive. Over time thinking in 3-D tactics could become natural, but especially for newer wraiths (especially of older persons more set in their perceptions) acting based on 2-D tactics - or at most looking for topographical advantage, would be familiar. Moving through solid objects may be possible, but is it probable for that character to abandon the familiar representations of physics as they find them psychological reinforcing.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>\u201cIt was clear to me for a long time that the origins of science had their deep roots in a particular myth, that of invariance.&quot; ~ Giorgio de Santillana<br /> Preface to Hamlet's Mill","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2345-whithers","name":"Whithers","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2345_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"141"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-14T20:33:55-08:00","id":370677,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I also saw some mention of the oceans being solid (I forget which thread). Is that canonical? Just wondering. Seems to me at odds with rain falling harmlessly through you, for example. </div> </div> </div>It is canonical and you can find the rules in Blood Dimmed Tides. Why rain passes harmlessly through you then? I'd say it is because oceans are rather enduring being more or less permanent features of the environment while rain is very much a temporal condition. It might also help that humanity as a whole has quite a lot of emotions tied into the oceans, though this is just my musings, making them a lot more real in the Shadowlands.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1629-possessed","name":"Possessed","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1629_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1140"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-15T06:35:40-08:00","id":370928,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>bphillips</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/370077-shadowland-physics#post370077\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think it was Crowley that mentioned a maximum thickness of two meters of dirt/stone that can impede progress. Is that a house rule? I don't remember seeing anything canonical addressing it, but it makes sense to have something preventing everyone from falling to the center of the earth.</div> </div> </div> </div>When i said that? O.o<br /> <br />","signatu